    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "2a848bdd-c1ed-3a38-8db0-48bb2b783c90",
                "word": "Bauhaus",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "888080ad-7c93-302a-a5f1-260059f2afb0",
                        "entry_id": "2a848bdd-c1ed-3a38-8db0-48bb2b783c90",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A modernist style characterized by the absence of ornamentation and by harmony between the function of a building or an object and its design."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "also",
                            "attributive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2facdcdd-4857-4988-912f-b3560977b5ae",
                                "sense_id": "888080ad-7c93-302a-a5f1-260059f2afb0",
                                "text": "[J]une 22 unanimous approval of the projected bauhaus building, the first of the bauhaus books appears."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ab8ebc44-d1de-40c4-9d1d-30cf15e48502",
                                "sense_id": "888080ad-7c93-302a-a5f1-260059f2afb0",
                                "text": "Not until the 1930's was the American public aware of the significance of the Bauhaus. Several factors contributed to this: American Bauhaus students were returning from Germany; Bauhaus design, as tubular steel chairs and lighting fixtures, were being imported from Germany; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6ff8d420-2c3c-41e0-8b6d-02f9c0ea21b9",
                                "sense_id": "888080ad-7c93-302a-a5f1-260059f2afb0",
                                "text": "While the direct impact of the iconoclastic Bauhaus typography on bookwork is limited, its indirect influence, encouraging designers such as Jan Tschichold to re-examine the whole tradition of typography and its relationship to twentieth-century life, is difficult to overestimate."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fc69ac3e-e947-4d2d-87f4-79fb0551cce9",
                                "sense_id": "888080ad-7c93-302a-a5f1-260059f2afb0",
                                "text": "The Bauhaus has long been celebrated as the most influential art school of the twentieth century. The word Bauhaus means many things to many people: some are inspired by so-called ‘Bauhaus style’ – from tubular-steel furniture to the strict geometry of primary-coloured triangles, circles and squares; others worship its position as one of the greatest emblems of the Modern movement’s ambitious desire to change the world."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "021e159e-a3a8-4066-a468-8eb2f28425ee",
                                "sense_id": "888080ad-7c93-302a-a5f1-260059f2afb0",
                                "text": "Two sites with cultural treasures separated by more than 40,000 years – caves with art dating to the Ice Age and buildings designed by a Bauhaus master less than 100 years ago – highlight Germany's submissions for the prestigious World Heritage Site designation by the U.N.'s cultural agency, UNESCO. […] The Bauhaus buildings in northeastern Germany were designed by the school's second director, Hannes Meyer."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
